    <h2>Home or in the chair?</h2>
    <p>Most of our patients choose home whitening: it is gentler, you control the pace, and you keep the trays for top-ups. In-chair whitening suits people who would rather get it done in one go, before a wedding, say. Either way, a dentist plans it and checks on you.</p>
    <h2>Why not just buy a kit?</h2>
    <p>Shop kits use one-size trays that let gel leak onto gums, and they cannot tell whether your discolouration is a stain, a dead tooth or an old filling. A quick check first often saves money and sore gums. Our post on <a href="/blog/is-teeth-whitening-safe/">whether whitening is safe</a> goes into more detail.</p>
    <h2>Keeping it bright</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Rinse with water after coffee, tea or red wine.</li>
        <li>Keep up regular cleanings: polishing lifts surface stains.</li>
        <li>Use your trays for a night or two every few months, if you like.</li>
    </ul>
